{"causes":["Emerging Sumerian city-states in Mesopotamia (c. 2900 BCE) led to the development of complex societies and governance structures.","The invention of cuneiform writing enabled the recording of stories, laws, and business transactions.","Ancient Mesopotamians adopted polytheistic religions, including the worship of gods like Enlil and Enki, which influenced their worldview and mythology."],"effects":["Established the epic poem as a literary genre in ancient Near East cultures.","Shaped the development of storytelling and oral traditions in ancient Greece.","Influenced the exploration of themes related to human nature, morality, and the consequences of actions."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Sumerian Civilization","sourceName":"Sumerian Civilization","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"This epic poem showcases the literary achievements of ancient Sumerians."},{"sourceSlug":"Akkadian Empire","sourceName":"Akkadian Empire","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"The Akkadians adopted and expanded upon Sumerian traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"Homer's Iliad","sourceName":"Homer's Iliad","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"Ancient Greek epic poetry was shaped by the themes and narrative structures found in The Epic of Gilgamesh."},{"sourceSlug":"Mesopotamia","sourceName":"Mesopotamia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"This epic poem originated in ancient Mesopotamia."},{"sourceSlug":"Enlil","sourceName":"Enlil","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"The Sumerians worshipped Enlil as a major god."},{"sourceSlug":"Uruk","sourceName":"Uruk","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"Gilgamesh was the king of Uruk, a city in ancient Mesopotamia."},{"sourceSlug":"Enkidu","sourceName":"Enkidu","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"Enkidu was the friend and companion of Gilgamesh in the epic poem."},{"sourceSlug":"Ancient Greece","sourceName":"Ancient Greece","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"Ancient Greek literature was influenced by the themes and narrative structures found in The Epic of Gilgamesh."},{"sourceSlug":"Enlilil","sourceName":"Enlilil","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Epic of Gilgamesh","targetName":"The Epic of Gilgamesh","context":"The Sumerians worshipped Enlil as a major god, which influenced their worldview and mythology."}],"places":[{"name":"Uruk","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Mesopotamia","role":"Located In"},{"name":"Nimrod's Valley","role":"Setting"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Epic of Gilgamesh","type":"Reference article","slug":"epic-of-gilgamesh"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q8272","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_of_Gilgamesh"],"tags":["literary work","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Epic of Gilgamesh is remembered as a landmark text of the Classical era, referenced in 131 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Epic of Gilgamesh is the oldest surviving work of epic literature in the world, composed in Sumerian and Akkadian cuneiform on clay tablets in ancient Mesopotamia between approximately 2100 and 1200 BCE. The poem centres on Gilgamesh, the historical king of Uruk (c. 2700 BCE) who was two-thirds divine and one-third human, and his companion Enkidu — a wild man formed from clay who becomes his closest friend. Together they slay the monster Humbaba in the Cedar Forest and kill the Bull of Heaven, but Enkidu's subsequent death sends Gilgamesh on a desperate search for immortality that ends at the ends of the earth, where the flood survivor Utnapishtim reveals that death cannot be conquered.

The epic's most celebrated section — the Flood Narrative in Tablet XI — describes a divine deluge sent to destroy humanity, survived by Utnapishtim who built a boat and preserved all living creatures, a story strikingly parallel to the later Biblical account of Noah. This discovery by George Smith in 1872 at the British Museum caused a sensation, demonstrating for the first time that Hebrew scripture shared sources with older Near Eastern literature. The Standard Babylonian Version, compiled by the scholar-scribe Sîn-lēqi-unninni around 1200 BCE, is the most complete recension, preserved across twelve tablets found at Ashurbanipal's library in Nineveh (destroyed 612 BCE).

The epic's themes — friendship, hubris, the fear of death, the limits of heroism, and the search for meaning — remain startlingly contemporary. It influenced the Odyssey, the Book of Job, and countless subsequent works. Its rediscovery in the 19th century transformed the study of ancient history and comparative religion, proving that civilisation's deepest preoccupations — mortality, companionship, and legacy — precede the Hebrew Bible by over a millennium.","subjects":["Literature","Mythology","Ancient History","Mesopotamia","Religion","Philosophy","Archaeology","Classical Era","Writing","Death and Immortality"],"frameworks":["CULTURAL_TRANSMISSION","COMPARATIVE_CIVILIZATIONS","LONGUE_DUREE"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Epic of Gilgamesh has had a profound impact on Western literary traditions and continues to explore complex moral and philosophical themes relevant today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
